    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-to-use" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. How to Use This Checklist</a>
          <a href="#after-every-session" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. After Every Session — 1 Minute</a>
          <a href="#monthly" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Monthly — 5 Minutes</a>
          <a href="#lubrication-interval" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Every 40-50 Hours of Use — 10 Minutes</a>
          <a href="#quarterly" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Quarterly — 5 Minutes</a>
          <a href="#annually" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Annually — 20 Minutes</a>
          <a href="#stop-signals" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. The Four Signals That Mean Stop</a>
          <a href="#when-something-changes" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. When Something Changes, Check These First</a>
          <a href="#record" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Keep a Simple Record</a>
          <a href="#environment" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Adjusting for Where the Machine Lives</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. The Whole Year, Summarised</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most treadmill maintenance advice is organised by component, which is useless when you
          are standing in front of the machine wondering what is due. This is organised by
          interval instead — work down it, and everything gets done at the right frequency.
        </p>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Use This Checklist</h2>
<p>
            Tasks are grouped by interval. The two that carry almost all the value are the
            after-session wipe and the 40-to-50-hour lubrication; everything else is a small
            addition to those.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where intervals are given in hours rather than months, that is deliberate. A machine
            used daily and one used fortnightly wear at completely different rates, and a
            calendar-based schedule either over-services one or neglects the other. Most consoles
            track cumulative hours under a maintenance or information menu — find that number
            once and the schedule becomes straightforward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Adjust the lubrication interval down to about 30 hours</strong> if the primary
            user is significantly above average weight, if two or more people use the machine
            daily, or if it lives somewhere dusty like a garage. Load and contamination both dry
            the belt-deck interface faster.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-every-session" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. After Every Session — 1 Minute</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Wipe down the belt surface, handrails and console.</strong> Sweat is mildly
            corrosive and will pit console surfaces and fasteners over time. A dry or barely damp
            cloth is enough; do not spray cleaner directly onto a console, where it can wick behind
            the panel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wipe the deck edges either side of the belt.</strong> This is where dust and
            fibre collect before being dragged into the belt-deck contact area, and it takes ten
            seconds.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Remove the safety key and store it away from the machine.</strong> This is a
            safety habit rather than maintenance — it prevents a child starting the machine — and
            it costs nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is the highest-value minute in the whole schedule because it prevents the
            accumulation that later becomes abrasive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="monthly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Monthly — 5 Minutes</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Vacuum around, beside and underneath the machine.</strong> Dust drawn toward
            the motor's cooling intake is the primary contaminant, and most of it is on the floor
            around the frame. If the machine folds, unfold it and vacuum the area it normally
            covers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the power cord and plug.</strong> Look for pinching where the cord passes
            under the frame, any discolouration at the plug, and heat at the socket after use.
            Any of those is a reason to stop and investigate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check frame fasteners by hand.</strong> Bolts at the upright-to-frame junction
            and around the handrails loosen gradually under vibration. A loose fastener is the most
            common cause of a rattle that people mistake for a mechanical fault, and a quarter turn
            with the supplied tool fixes it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the belt edges</strong> for fraying or any sign of the layers
            separating. Early delamination is far easier to act on than late.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lubrication-interval" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Every 40-50 Hours of Use — 10 Minutes</h2>
<p>
            This is the task that determines whether the machine reaches five years or ten. Rising
            belt-to-deck friction makes the motor draw more current, and sustained current draw is
            what destroys motors and controller boards.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the deck by hand first.</strong> Unplugged, reach under the belt near the
            centre and run a finger along the deck. Slightly slick is correct. Dry, warm or gritty
            means lubricate now regardless of the hour count.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clean before applying.</strong> Wipe the deck and belt underside with a
            lint-free cloth lightly dampened in isopropyl alcohol. Applying fresh lubricant over
            old residue traps an abrasive slurry against the deck, which is the opposite of the
            intent.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Apply about an ounce of 100% silicone</strong> along the centre third of the
            deck, under the belt, then <strong>walk on it slowly for three to five minutes</strong>
            to distribute it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            has the full procedure and
            <a href="/best-treadmill-lubricant/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the lubricant guide</a>
            covers which product to buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quarterly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Quarterly — 5 Minutes</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Check belt tension.</strong> With the machine off, lift the belt at the centre
            of the deck. It should rise about two to three inches. Less means it is over-tightened,
            which loads the rollers, bearings and motor unnecessarily; more means it may slip under
            load. Our
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a>
            covers the adjustment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check belt tracking.</strong> Run the machine at a walking pace, unloaded, and
            watch whether the belt sits centred or drifts toward one rail. Minor drift is normal and
            adjustable; steady migration needs correcting before it damages the belt edge. Our
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring guide</a>
            covers the quarter-turn method.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Test the safety key.</strong> Start the belt at a slow speed and pull the key.
            It should stop promptly. This is the one feature that helps at the moment something
            goes wrong and it is worth confirming it works rather than assuming.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the incline mechanism</strong> through its full range, listening for
            grinding or hesitation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="annually" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Annually — 20 Minutes</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Vacuum the motor compartment.</strong> With the machine unplugged, remove the
            motor hood and vacuum the accumulated dust, using a soft brush attachment and not
            touching the board. This is the task most owners never do and it directly affects motor
            cooling. Our
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor compartment guide</a>
            covers doing it safely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clean thoroughly underneath the belt.</strong> More than the lubrication wipe —
            the full deck surface, both sides, with the belt slackened. Our
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to cleaning under the belt</a>
            covers the method.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Inspect the deck surface</strong> for polishing, shine or grooves under the belt
            path. That indicates the low-friction layer is wearing through. Many decks are
            reversible, and flipping one effectively doubles deck life for the cost of labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Turn each roller by hand</strong> with the belt slack, feeling for roughness or
            a catch. Smooth and silent is what you want; gritty means bearings are wearing and you
            have useful advance warning.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the belt for wear</strong> — thinning at the centre, fraying at the
            edges, or any separation of the layers. Our
            <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt replacement guide</a>
            covers when it is due.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stop-signals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Four Signals That Mean Stop</h2>
<p>
            These are not maintenance items. If any of them appears, stop using the machine and get
            it assessed by someone qualified.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A burning smell or hot-plastic odour.</strong> Overheating insulation or a
            failing component. Unplug it and do not run it again to see if it recurs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Visible sparking</strong>, anywhere on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt moving when the machine is off.</strong> This indicates a fault in the
            motor control circuit and means the machine can start unexpectedly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Any smoke</strong>, however slight.
          </p>
          <p>
            A repeatedly tripping breaker belongs on this list too. It is a symptom of excessive
            current draw, and resetting it repeatedly is treating the alarm rather than the fire.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            covers diagnosing faults that are safe to investigate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-something-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. When Something Changes, Check These First</h2>
<p>
            Outside the schedule, three symptoms have a standard first response that resolves them
            most of the time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt hesitates when you step on it.</strong> Friction, four times out of
            five. Lubricate before considering anything else, and specifically before tightening
            the belt — tightening a slipping belt is usually the wrong first move and adds load to
            the motor. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to belt slowing</a>
            covers the fix order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A new noise.</strong> Locate it before treating it — belt-deck, rollers, motor
            drive belt and frame fasteners all sound different and need different fixes. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">noise guide</a>
            covers the three questions that locate any treadmill noise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt drifts to one side.</strong> Tracking adjustment, usually a quarter
            turn on one rear roller bolt and then thirty seconds of waiting that most people skip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="record" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Keep a Simple Record</h2>
<p>
            A note of what you did and when takes seconds and pays off twice.
          </p>
          <p>
            It tells you when lubrication is next due without guessing, which is the difference
            between a schedule and good intentions. Taping an index card to the side of the frame
            and writing the date and console hours at each lubrication is enough — no app required.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also matters for warranty. Some manufacturers can decline a motor claim where a deck
            was clearly never lubricated, and a dated record is the simplest possible rebuttal. On a
            repair costing several hundred dollars, that card has earned its place.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you sell the machine, a maintenance record measurably improves what it fetches
            and how quickly it sells, because the first thing an informed buyer asks is how often it
            was lubricated. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used buying checklist</a>
            is written from the other side of that conversation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="environment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Adjusting for Where the Machine Lives</h2>
<p>
            The schedule above assumes a treadmill in a normal heated room. Three environments
            change it, and all three shorten intervals rather than lengthening them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Garages and unheated rooms.</strong> The biggest single adjustment. Cold
            thickens lubricant so the motor works harder for the first several minutes of every
            session, and temperature swings drive condensation onto metal surfaces and the
            controller board. Garages are also dusty. Halve the lubrication interval, vacuum
            fortnightly rather than monthly, and let the machine sit at room temperature before a
            session if you can. Where the space is genuinely cold, a fitted cover between uses is
            worth more than it costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Carpeted rooms.</strong> Carpet pile blocks the motor's cooling airflow and
            sheds fibre continuously, and fibre drawn into the motor housing is the fastest route
            to a heat problem. If the machine must sit on carpet, a rigid mat between the two
            solves most of it — and bring the annual motor-compartment vacuum forward to every six
            months. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills on carpet</a>
            covers the full set of issues.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Humid rooms, basements and conservatories.</strong> Moisture and metal are a
            poor combination over years. Check fasteners and the cord entry for corrosion at each
            monthly pass, and keep the machine off a concrete floor directly — a mat provides both
            a moisture break and vibration isolation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Whole Year, Summarised</h2>
<p>
            <strong>After every session (1 min):</strong> wipe belt, handrails, console and deck
            edges. Remove the safety key.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Monthly (5 min):</strong> vacuum around and under the machine, check the cord
            and plug, check frame fasteners, inspect belt edges.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Every 40-50 hours (10 min):</strong> check the deck by hand, clean, lubricate
            with 100% silicone, walk it in.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Quarterly (5 min):</strong> check belt tension and tracking, test the safety
            key, run the incline through its range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Annually (20 min):</strong> vacuum the motor compartment, clean fully under the
            belt, inspect the deck surface and rollers, assess belt wear.
          </p>
          <p>
            Totalled: roughly 50 to 70 minutes a year and about $20 of consumables, against a
            machine lifespan that roughly doubles. It is the best-value hour in home fitness.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I do treadmill maintenance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wipe down after every session, vacuum around the machine monthly, lubricate every 40 to 50 hours of use, check belt tension and tracking quarterly, and do a full inspection annually. The whole year totals under an hour and roughly $20 of consumables.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most important treadmill maintenance task?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lubricating the belt every 40 to 50 hours of use. Rising belt-to-deck friction makes the motor draw more current, and sustained current draw is what destroys motors and controller boards — the two most expensive components. It is a ten-minute job that most owners never do once.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to maintain a treadmill if I barely use it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less often, because the interval is measured in running hours rather than months. But a machine that sits unused still collects dust, and the belt-deck interface still dries out slowly. Check the deck by hand every few months — slightly slick is correct, dry or gritty means lubricate regardless of hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know how many hours my treadmill has done?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most consoles track cumulative hours under a maintenance, service or information menu — the manual will say how to reach it, and it is often a button combination held for several seconds. Find it once and the hour-based intervals in this checklist become straightforward to follow.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I keep a treadmill maintenance record?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and an index card taped to the frame is enough. It tells you when lubrication is next due, it is the simplest rebuttal if a manufacturer questions maintenance during a warranty claim, and it measurably improves resale value because informed buyers ask how often it was lubricated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I stop using my treadmill and call someone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Four signals: a burning or hot-plastic smell, visible sparking, the belt moving when the machine is off, or any smoke. A repeatedly tripping breaker belongs on that list too. These are not maintenance problems and repeatedly resetting or restarting is treating the alarm rather than the fault.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Following a Maintenance Schedule</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Three of these are about the interval and one is about the order of operations, which is where most well-intentioned maintenance goes wrong.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a calendar interval instead of running hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine used daily and one used fortnightly wear at completely different rates, so "every three months" either over-services one or neglects the other. Most consoles track cumulative hours under a maintenance menu. Find that number once and lubricate every 40 to 50 hours — 30 for heavier or multi-user households.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lubricating without cleaning first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Old lubricant is full of dust, carpet fibre and belt debris. Fresh silicone applied over it traps an abrasive slurry directly against the deck, which is exactly the friction you were trying to remove. Wipe the deck and belt underside with isopropyl alcohol on a lint-free cloth and let it dry fully.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening a slipping belt before lubricating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A belt that hesitates under load is usually reporting friction, not slack. Tightening it increases load on the rollers, bearings and motor while leaving the actual cause in place. Lubricate first, run it, and only then reassess tension — the symptom resolves without adjustment most of the time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never opening the motor hood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The annual motor-compartment vacuum is the task owners skip most, and dust accumulation there directly reduces motor cooling on the component least able to tolerate heat. With the machine unplugged and a soft brush attachment, it takes ten minutes and does not require touching anything electrical.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

